WEBVTT

00:00:02.590 --> 00:00:06.970
 At first, it was a prize at the SF MoMA, an art prize that I had gotten.

00:00:06.970 --> 00:00:10.150
 At that time, I was pretty disillusioned with the art world.

00:00:10.150 --> 00:00:13.920
 I was beginning to be disillusioned with San Francisco at that point.

00:00:13.920 --> 00:00:16.070
 It felt very apolitical.

00:00:16.070 --> 00:00:19.980
 And I was like, you know, if I'm going to put a lot of work into an art project, which I do,

00:00:19.980 --> 00:00:25.040
 I wanted to do something more than just sit in a gallery with a very small audience.

00:00:25.040 --> 00:00:32.000
 But I was like, you know what, I actually want to do something for this city as an attempt to make it a livable city that I want to stay in.

00:00:32.000 --> 00:00:34.180
 So it's kind of selfish, but...

00:00:34.180 --> 00:00:41.180
 And so I had heard about victory gardens in a beautiful book called City Bountiful by Laura Lawson.

00:00:41.180 --> 00:00:47.360
 And it said in her book that San Francisco was the most successful of all of the programs across the United States.

00:00:47.360 --> 00:00:52.700
 So I went to the history library in San Francisco, and they had a folder called Victory Garden.

00:00:52.700 --> 00:00:56.900
 And in that folder was a picture of a farm in front of City Hall, San Francisco.

00:00:56.900 --> 00:01:00.700
 And I just was like, why don't we have a garden, a farm in front of City Hall now?

00:01:00.700 --> 00:01:02.730
 Why do we have turf?

00:01:02.730 --> 00:01:04.170
 So I had kind of like a spark of idea.

00:01:04.170 --> 00:01:17.890
 I want to propose a contemporary version of Victory Gardens to the city of San Francisco and use my little stipend from the SF MoMA to give away some gardens and then also use some time to see if we can get the city to have a garden in front of City Hall.

00:01:17.890 --> 00:01:27.350
 Welcome to Delicious Revolution, a show about food, culture, and place.

00:01:27.590 --> 00:01:28.310
 I'm Chelsea Wills.

00:01:28.510 --> 00:01:34.450
 This season, we're talking with visionary chefs, gardeners, farmers, organizers, artists, and scientists.

00:01:34.940 --> 00:01:37.780
 These people have shaped the food movement in California.

00:01:38.240 --> 00:01:48.420
 We talk with a diverse group of California's rebel food makers about the ways they do things in their farms, kitchens, and communities that reshape the way we think about food.

00:01:48.420 --> 00:01:51.260
 This show is made by Devin Sampson and Chelsea Wills.

00:01:51.480 --> 00:01:57.860
 Special thanks to the support from Cal Humanities, Food First, and Rebecca Murillo for making this season possible.

00:01:57.860 --> 00:02:10.620
 Amy Franceschini is an artist and educator who creates formats for exchange and production that question and challenge the social, cultural, and environmental systems that surround her.

00:02:10.880 --> 00:02:20.060
 An overarching theme in her work is a perceived conflict between humans and nature, and her projects reveal the ways that local politics are affected by globalization.

00:02:20.660 --> 00:02:23.540
 In 1995, Amy founded Future Farmers.

00:02:23.540 --> 00:02:35.130
 She is currently sailing from Oslo to Istanbul as a part of Seed Journey, bringing seeds found in Norway and other points in the Northern Hemisphere to their center of origin in the Middle East,

00:02:35.570 --> 00:02:40.330
 and connecting with seed savers, farmers, bakers, activists, and others along the way.

00:02:40.940 --> 00:02:41.920
 Hi, Amy.

00:02:42.360 --> 00:02:43.050
 Hello.

00:02:43.670 --> 00:02:44.970
 Welcome to the show.

00:02:45.930 --> 00:02:47.310
 Thanks for having me.

00:02:47.310 --> 00:02:52.440
 You're in London right now, getting ready for this new big project called Seed Journey.

00:02:53.330 --> 00:02:55.270
 And maybe we can start there.

00:02:55.350 --> 00:02:58.530
 Maybe you can tell me a little bit about what the Seed Journey is.

00:02:59.140 --> 00:02:59.340
 Yep.

00:02:59.690 --> 00:03:13.390
 So Seed Journey is a year-long seafaring movement through time and space with a cargo of a small handful of ancient grains that we've been growing in Oslo for the last three years.

00:03:13.710 --> 00:03:25.160
 And these ancient grains have been found in different places, sometimes very strange places, like between two boards in an old sauna in the north of Norway.

00:03:25.300 --> 00:03:33.850
 Nine of these seeds were found in the 1970s and planted, and seven of them came back to life and have been brought into a land race.

00:03:34.630 --> 00:03:40.720
 And we've been collecting a selection of these seeds over the last three years.

00:03:40.720 --> 00:03:45.020
 And speaking of imagination, it was just kind of a passing fantasy of mine.

00:03:45.100 --> 00:03:50.330
 And I said, wow, all these people have origin stories of the seeds that we're growing, and they're always different.

00:03:50.850 --> 00:04:00.100
 And I was like, maybe we should take them back to where they were domesticated or where they're theoretically domesticated in the fertile crescent in the Middle East.

00:04:00.360 --> 00:04:10.670
 And along that path, meet other seed savers and grain growers and bakers and small-scale producers and have actually the grains be our compass.

00:04:11.730 --> 00:04:20.340
 So we do have a fixed journey, which is from Oslo, leaving this September 17th, to Istanbul, and we'll arrive in one year in Istanbul.

00:04:21.060 --> 00:04:32.370
 We have some stops along the way, but basically what's happened is it's garnered so much interest that we keep getting invitations from different farmers and land worker alliances or commons,

00:04:32.590 --> 00:04:41.380
 common people who are working on ideas around the commons, that we've pretty much just opened ourselves up to being pulled where the wind will take us.

00:04:41.760 --> 00:04:48.640
 So I'm in London right now preparing for our first stop, which will arrive here three weeks after we leave Oslo.

00:04:48.860 --> 00:04:58.230
 And we will put our boat in the Thames River and continue on to Cardiff, where we have our first exhibition of this project at the National Museum in Cardiff, Wales.

00:04:59.110 --> 00:05:01.170
 Wow, there's a lot of things to ask about with that.

00:05:01.170 --> 00:05:04.510
 So what's an ancient grain? What's this idea of an ancient grain?

00:05:04.610 --> 00:05:10.750
 That's a good question. So it's a problematic term. Sometimes these grains are called ancient grains.

00:05:10.930 --> 00:05:14.630
 Sometimes they're called heritage grains. Sometimes they're called cultural grains.

00:05:15.480 --> 00:05:22.270
 I like to call them social media. I think they're like the most high-tech, robust high-tech mediums we have in our lives.

00:05:22.270 --> 00:05:34.210
 But basically what they're referring to when they say ancient or heritage is that these are grains that were in production before industrial agriculture came into play.

00:05:34.370 --> 00:05:45.320
 So most of them fell out of production because they weren't selected for traits of high starch or strength and durability in certain types of weather.

00:05:45.980 --> 00:05:55.190
 And in a way that's a good thing because they have kept all these different genetic traits and haven't been turned into homogenous foodstuffs.

00:05:56.010 --> 00:06:02.730
 So they kind of fell out of production between the late 1800s and the early 1930s.

00:06:02.770 --> 00:06:15.590
 And in the 70s, people started to kind of remember these seeds because the industrial grains were failing and people were starting to already get stomach problems and gluten intolerance.

00:06:15.810 --> 00:06:25.320
 And so a small group of farmers around the world either were continuing to grow these, but just on a very small scale, or they started growing them again.

00:06:26.100 --> 00:06:35.530
 And so the idea is that they have kind of kept the qualities of a place in which they were grown in the last time they were grown.

00:06:36.080 --> 00:06:39.470
 So there's a certain heritage to them related to place.

00:06:39.900 --> 00:06:41.470
 Does that answer your question?

00:06:42.470 --> 00:06:46.040
 Yeah, I think, well, we could talk about that for the whole hour.

00:06:46.040 --> 00:06:49.420
 There's a lot to say just about that all on its own.

00:06:50.200 --> 00:06:57.500
 When I thought about interviewing you and when I think about your work, I think there's like this big relationship to place.

00:06:58.740 --> 00:07:04.660
 And it seems to me like you're kind of playing with this idea of imagination and place and food.

00:07:05.270 --> 00:07:07.170
 Who are the people who are cultivating these grains?

00:07:07.330 --> 00:07:08.890
 What's happening with them?

00:07:10.090 --> 00:07:12.330
 Why do they need to go back to the Fertile Crescent?

00:07:13.130 --> 00:07:15.490
 I mean, I think the idea is a little bit absurd.

00:07:15.990 --> 00:07:18.750
 And I think that's why we're doing it.

00:07:18.850 --> 00:07:19.450
 It's kind of a provocation.

00:07:20.450 --> 00:07:32.950
 And it's actually, I've found that the language around this idea of returning them or returning to their origins has a lot of challenges to it.

00:07:33.210 --> 00:07:39.460
 And I think it provokes conversations not only about biological matter, but social histories.

00:07:40.370 --> 00:07:45.170
 And I think that's the exciting thing about the questions that underlie the journey.

00:07:45.830 --> 00:07:49.280
 But you asked about the people that are growing these grains.

00:07:49.280 --> 00:07:53.810
 And they're usually, all of them that I've met are heroes.

00:07:54.690 --> 00:07:59.900
 Most of them are middle-aged men that are found or very old men.

00:08:00.690 --> 00:08:09.430
 In Italy, we've worked with a lot of ancient grain farmers who are in their late 70s and early 80s who have been growing these grains forever.

00:08:09.630 --> 00:08:11.290
 Like, they never stopped producing them.

00:08:11.390 --> 00:08:14.180
 But this is a very small region in Abruzzo.

00:08:15.220 --> 00:08:20.880
 And technically, it's illegal for them to sell these grains, according to EU law.

00:08:21.130 --> 00:08:22.290
 But they're going to continue.

00:08:22.650 --> 00:08:34.060
 And a lot of the grain farmers have formed coalitions sort of to protect themselves and to say, hey, let's not just be alone doing this endeavor.

00:08:34.220 --> 00:08:38.510
 Let's work together and try to empower this movement.

00:08:39.400 --> 00:08:49.660
 Also, a lot of these grains aren't allowed into bigger mills because they're considered dirty or, you know, a lot of the farmers intercrop.

00:08:49.660 --> 00:08:54.640
 So, they're not just growing straight-up grains that intercrop with clover or other green manure.

00:08:55.100 --> 00:08:59.560
 So, they don't clean the bundles when they take them to the mills.

00:08:59.560 --> 00:09:00.680
 So, they're not really allowed.

00:09:00.840 --> 00:09:08.810
 So, a lot of, actually, the movement has grown through shared mills because people have to meet.

00:09:08.930 --> 00:09:09.970
 They have to talk to each other.

00:09:10.690 --> 00:09:14.370
 And that challenge has actually strengthened the movement a lot.

00:09:15.430 --> 00:09:16.570
 What are some of these grains?

00:09:17.760 --> 00:09:20.700
 So, my favorite grain is a Finnish rye.

00:09:20.700 --> 00:09:22.140
 It's called Svedgerug.

00:09:22.660 --> 00:09:25.940
 And that word means swidden rye.

00:09:26.100 --> 00:09:32.870
 And swidden was the practice of slash and burn of basically a group of people called the forest fins.

00:09:33.410 --> 00:09:40.080
 And they would go to a forest and cut it down and grow food for about 10 years and then move on.

00:09:40.080 --> 00:09:44.770
 And on a small scale, that's actually a great agricultural practice for the land.

00:09:46.300 --> 00:09:57.040
 And this rye, the last time it was produced in Norway, which in the 1800s, Finnish people were still farming in the north of Norway.

00:09:57.640 --> 00:10:01.140
 These were kind of, kind of fell out of production in the late 1800s.

00:10:02.350 --> 00:10:09.010
 And in 1970, an amateur historian had read about this, a man named Per Martin Spensberg.

00:10:09.450 --> 00:10:14.070
 And he went to a place where they, where it was thought that they had been farming.

00:10:14.310 --> 00:10:18.530
 And he found an old sauna, which is where they used to dry the grains in the roof.

00:10:19.130 --> 00:10:24.230
 And he got permission to take apart, he looked around for seeds, but then there were no seeds.

00:10:24.310 --> 00:10:27.830
 So, he got permission to take apart the roof where it was thought that they dried them.

00:10:27.970 --> 00:10:29.290
 And he found these nine grains.

00:10:29.950 --> 00:10:35.300
 And when they planted them, he got permission to plant them in the church in the same town.

00:10:36.330 --> 00:10:40.450
 Seven of these rye grew, and they grew to three meters tall.

00:10:41.510 --> 00:10:44.290
 And they're a biennial, so it takes them two years to grow.

00:10:45.230 --> 00:10:47.770
 And from one seed, you can get 1,200 seeds.

00:10:48.560 --> 00:10:52.160
 So, the first year, it grows into 12 little tufts of grass.

00:10:52.710 --> 00:10:59.500
 And then the second year, each of those tufts grows a stalk with a hundred or sometimes 200 seeds on them.

00:11:00.010 --> 00:11:05.390
 So, you can imagine you get, you know, 2,400 or 1,200 seeds from one seed.

00:11:06.170 --> 00:11:09.850
 It's also a beautiful blue, bluish color.

00:11:10.660 --> 00:11:15.570
 And it's very tasty, and it has hardly any gluten.

00:11:16.480 --> 00:11:24.860
 And it's also very durable in floods, because Oslo's been experiencing, or Norway's been experiencing way more rain than normal.

00:11:25.020 --> 00:11:28.020
 And a lot of the industrial rye is just falling over.

00:11:28.020 --> 00:11:32.730
 So, this rye is resilient, and it's just amazing.

00:11:32.910 --> 00:11:38.580
 So, that's one of my favorite ryes, or grains.

00:11:38.880 --> 00:11:47.460
 Another one is, it's a barley that fell out of production in the 30s.

00:11:47.910 --> 00:11:52.130
 And it was, some seeds were sent to the Vavilov Institute in St. Petersburg.

00:11:52.520 --> 00:11:53.600
 Are you familiar with that?

00:11:54.050 --> 00:11:54.290
 Yeah.

00:11:54.750 --> 00:12:00.880
 And they were sent by Fritjof Nansen, who was a national hero of Norway, an explorer.

00:12:02.310 --> 00:12:04.250
 And he sent them there to save them.

00:12:04.330 --> 00:12:05.910
 He knew that these seeds were special.

00:12:06.370 --> 00:12:07.750
 And then it was forgotten.

00:12:08.470 --> 00:12:24.610
 And I read a book by Gary Nabhan, and there's a little vignette in there about these scientists during the siege of Leningrad, who were assigned to keep saving seeds during the war, and to lock all the doors and save the seeds from the starving population.

00:12:25.490 --> 00:12:35.790
 And when the allied troops came, they found these 12 scientists with seeds stuffed in their pockets, and they found them stuffed in the walls.

00:12:36.520 --> 00:12:42.110
 And I thought, maybe some of those seeds were these seeds that were sent from Norway.

00:12:42.700 --> 00:12:47.560
 So, a friend of ours went to the Vavilov Institute and worked with a biologist there.

00:12:47.740 --> 00:12:51.450
 And he was so excited that we might grow these seeds.

00:12:51.900 --> 00:12:54.690
 And he found, he ended up finding eight varieties of grains.

00:12:54.690 --> 00:13:08.570
 But the barley was the most exciting because, actually, Norwegians, when they were, like, starving or when there was a really rough year, they would eat only barley flatbread.

00:13:09.000 --> 00:13:12.830
 So, this is a barley that, actually, Norway was really excited to have back.

00:13:12.910 --> 00:13:15.890
 So, we brought it back, and we've been growing it for three years.

00:13:16.230 --> 00:13:21.910
 And we don't have enough to make a significant amount of bread yet, but it's growing in population.

00:13:21.910 --> 00:13:29.620
 So, that's really an exciting grain that we've gotten access through, through this project.

00:13:31.260 --> 00:13:33.720
 Well, those are two really good stories.

00:13:33.980 --> 00:13:41.160
 Jumping into this world of looking for these ancient grains, have there been a lot of people who have come forward with stories like this?

00:13:42.170 --> 00:13:49.020
 Yeah, there's, I mean, it's, that's the exciting thing about the project, or that's, it's actually the exciting thing about making work.

00:13:49.720 --> 00:13:53.520
 It's like you talk about it, you have a reason to talk about something, and then people are like,

00:13:53.520 --> 00:14:00.340
 oh, someone brought me a seed from Istanbul called the black beard, and they think it's the first grain to be domesticated.

00:14:00.620 --> 00:14:09.020
 Or, you know, like, we have a whole library now of gifts that we've been given with really beautiful stories connected to them.

00:14:11.220 --> 00:14:20.070
 My work has been in Mexico, mostly in southern Mexico, where there's a lot of different varieties of corn, and it sounds very similar.

00:14:20.070 --> 00:14:25.990
 So, you know, we're, you know, tracing these, like, strange, like, a lot of strange sets of circumstances through these plants.

00:14:26.630 --> 00:14:26.730
 Yeah.

00:14:26.730 --> 00:14:35.400
 And, well, you mentioned quickly this idea of the commons, and thinking about seed banks, especially.

00:14:36.340 --> 00:14:40.400
 And maybe could you talk a little bit about what you think about seed banks?

00:14:40.460 --> 00:14:43.590
 I think there's a lot to kind of unpack in that.

00:14:44.870 --> 00:14:52.760
 So, the farmer we've been working with in Oslo gave us access to these finished ryes, and most of the grains we're growing.

00:14:53.200 --> 00:14:54.380
 His name is Johan Svart.

00:14:55.170 --> 00:15:00.230
 And he said something that we've adopted as kind of a motto for Seed Journey, which is,

00:15:00.760 --> 00:15:03.380
 we don't need museums to conserve varieties.

00:15:03.800 --> 00:15:05.720
 What we want to do is grow them in the soil.

00:15:06.910 --> 00:15:12.580
 And he considers seed banks very essential, but they're more like museums.

00:15:13.540 --> 00:15:18.570
 They're a place that their job is to conserve these in case of, you know, emergencies.

00:15:19.840 --> 00:15:23.840
 And Gene Banks' job are to preserve a pure race of a line.

00:15:25.030 --> 00:15:31.860
 But equally important or more important is to grow these seeds out in the world every single year,

00:15:31.860 --> 00:15:36.880
 so that they're adapting to the environment and to the social needs of the people who are eating them.

00:15:39.430 --> 00:15:42.330
 And I think, you know, I think both are important.

00:15:42.610 --> 00:15:50.170
 And I think the main crux of probably the work you're doing, too, is about these questions of coexistence.

00:15:50.350 --> 00:15:54.980
 Like, okay, we're tons of people on this planet, and we have to eat.

00:15:55.530 --> 00:16:05.580
 And we have to be mindful of possible disasters, environmental or social disasters that could endanger our food supply.

00:16:06.780 --> 00:16:09.980
 So, we have to have a diversified practice.

00:16:10.620 --> 00:16:24.490
 I don't necessarily agree with, you know, how much we spend on seed banks and who has access to them and who probably has control over them.

00:16:24.550 --> 00:16:30.600
 I think that's the biggest problem for me is, in the end, who has control over the seed banks.

00:16:30.600 --> 00:16:44.270
 Well, I think there's this idea of, you know, situated preservation of seeds, right, that exist in a whole complex ecosystem that is both an environmental ecosystem,

00:16:44.470 --> 00:16:49.140
 but also definitely like a cultural and knowledge-based ecosystem.

00:16:50.100 --> 00:16:55.220
 And I, like you, am not opposed to seed banks as an idea.

00:16:55.380 --> 00:17:05.560
 I mean, part of it sort of really touches this part of my imagination that loves science fiction fixes to things.

00:17:05.700 --> 00:17:13.410
 I think there's this idea somehow that our salvation is all stored away in some crypt somewhere.

00:17:14.190 --> 00:17:14.350
 Yeah.

00:17:14.730 --> 00:17:27.830
 You know, but the reality is that without people knowing how to produce, prepare, and talk about these foods, we can't eat them, right?

00:17:27.890 --> 00:17:27.990
 Yeah.

00:17:28.320 --> 00:17:35.440
 So maybe we can kind of circle back around with your work.

00:17:35.540 --> 00:17:37.320
 How did you start working with food?

00:17:37.500 --> 00:17:41.890
 And, I mean, I feel like you come at this from so many different angles at once, right?

00:17:42.440 --> 00:17:56.680
 And, well, that's what feels interesting to me is it doesn't feel, it doesn't feel, there's so many layers to it that it actually feels like the complexity of what it is to kind of continue to explore these systems.

00:17:57.310 --> 00:17:57.710
 Yeah.

00:17:57.950 --> 00:17:58.150
 Right?

00:17:58.190 --> 00:18:03.750
 It's confusing and amazing, and there's not a simple solution to it.

00:18:04.580 --> 00:18:05.100
 No.

00:18:05.100 --> 00:18:12.100
 And I think, I think it takes a lot of energy to hover in the complexity and not just choose one simple story.

00:18:12.420 --> 00:18:20.240
 And it definitely works for us in some ways, but also, like, against us, like, in terms of funding of our work.

00:18:20.460 --> 00:18:24.010
 You know, people want a quick, simple sentence description of what you do.

00:18:24.610 --> 00:18:25.960
 And that's tricky.

00:18:26.100 --> 00:18:34.380
 But how I got into this was I grew up in the Central Valley of California, in the San Joaquin Valley.

00:18:34.380 --> 00:18:45.300
 And my father was an industrial farmer, and he grew commodity crops and wheat, barley, oats, almonds.

00:18:45.740 --> 00:18:48.040
 Walnuts, you know, everything you could think of.

00:18:48.770 --> 00:18:50.800
 And he also owned a pesticide company.

00:18:51.650 --> 00:19:00.770
 And when I was about between three and four years old, every time the crop duster would come and crop dust our fields, I would get really, really sick and I couldn't breathe.

00:19:01.010 --> 00:19:07.420
 And I'd have to go to the hospital and get injections to open my lungs, two different types of injections.

00:19:07.810 --> 00:19:13.590
 And my mother was starting to study Rudolf Steiner and permaculture and biodynamic farming.

00:19:14.130 --> 00:19:19.290
 And she started to put pieces together and was like, you know, I think this is not a health environment for my daughter.

00:19:19.790 --> 00:19:22.370
 And long story short, they got divorced.

00:19:22.530 --> 00:19:28.140
 And my mom became an activist fighting really hard against the use of chemical pesticides.

00:19:28.700 --> 00:19:37.870
 We moved to San Luis Obispo County, where she fought for 12 years to have strawberry farmers stop using malethion.

00:19:38.350 --> 00:19:43.610
 And a group of people were successful in transitioning several farms into organic farms.

00:19:44.450 --> 00:19:56.510
 Then I'd spend the summers with my father in the tractor, in the truck, driving through 4,000 acres of row crops and different shifts and types of pesticides and fungicides.

00:19:56.790 --> 00:20:11.900
 And so the thing that I kind of brought away from this as an art student was that both of them had completely different ideologies politically, socially, and in practice as farmers.

00:20:11.900 --> 00:20:15.550
 But they both thought they were growing good food for people.

00:20:15.860 --> 00:20:19.530
 My dad thought he was feeding the world, which he was in a way.

00:20:19.970 --> 00:20:23.190
 You know that industrial agriculture is feeding a lot of people.

00:20:23.620 --> 00:20:26.520
 And my mother thought she was feeding people good food.

00:20:27.530 --> 00:20:32.430
 And that food was a way to bring people with different ideologies together.

00:20:32.430 --> 00:20:42.490
 And that I wanted to be committed to creating platforms where people from different classes and race feel comfortable together.

00:20:42.840 --> 00:20:49.760
 And can decide to put down their differences and figure out how to navigate through some tough waters.

00:20:50.420 --> 00:20:54.090
 I'm not saying that we're super successful in that.

00:20:54.170 --> 00:21:01.650
 But it's definitely been part of what we try to do with our work.

00:21:01.650 --> 00:21:09.950
 It's like create open platforms where people from different walks of life can come together to try to change things.

00:21:12.590 --> 00:21:14.130
 That's a pretty big goal.

00:21:14.690 --> 00:21:17.250
 And right?

00:21:17.490 --> 00:21:18.410
 I mean, it is.

00:21:18.510 --> 00:21:25.950
 It's, I think, but I think food is maybe one of the only places that has that space in it.

00:21:26.150 --> 00:21:26.330
 Yeah.

00:21:26.670 --> 00:21:29.610
 Food and maybe creativity also.

00:21:30.220 --> 00:21:30.800
 Yeah.

00:21:30.840 --> 00:21:43.650
 I mean, I think, yeah, I mean, we've, I've, maybe we've just been lucky because I feel like about five of our projects have been really successful in somehow bringing kind of high,

00:21:44.050 --> 00:21:54.110
 high-end politicians and grassroots organizers together and urban planners to kind of agree on some sort of food movement or food systems.

00:21:54.110 --> 00:22:05.410
 And I think it has had a lot to do with kind of having the imagination to use underutilized resources or to find kind of cracks in systems.

00:22:06.800 --> 00:22:14.030
 And, yeah, I think we've had good luck and that's what's given us wind to keep, keep going and try to do this.

00:22:15.020 --> 00:22:18.020
 Is, is the Victory Gardens project one of those projects?

00:22:18.860 --> 00:22:19.820
 Yeah, definitely.

00:22:20.880 --> 00:22:23.180
 Could you just describe what that was?

00:22:23.260 --> 00:22:31.330
 I've, I think about, I was living in San Francisco at that time and, um, my partner, Devin, helped make gardens with you.

00:22:31.870 --> 00:22:32.990
 And, yeah.

00:22:33.150 --> 00:22:35.790
 And Brooke and Brooke Budner.

00:22:36.170 --> 00:22:43.450
 And, um, I remember that feeling like a really hopeful moment in San Francisco.

00:22:44.140 --> 00:22:44.700
 Yes.

00:22:44.880 --> 00:22:50.700
 Um, in a way that there's been a lot of transformation from that moment.

00:22:50.700 --> 00:23:03.500
 And it's also a totally different city now, um, where I think that that hopefulness or that feeling of opportunity is really, feels much smaller than it was at that time.

00:23:03.920 --> 00:23:13.050
 But, um, how did that project come about and what did you, it leveraged so many different things at once as far as I remember it.

00:23:13.860 --> 00:23:20.700
 Yeah, it was, you know, it was, at first it was a prize at the SF MoMA, an art prize that I had gotten.

00:23:21.430 --> 00:23:26.310
 And when I got it at that time, I was pretty disillusioned with the art world.

00:23:26.450 --> 00:23:29.970
 I was beginning to be disillusioned with San Francisco at that point.

00:23:30.150 --> 00:23:31.630
 It felt very apolitical.

00:23:31.770 --> 00:23:42.400
 And I was like, you know, if I'm going to put a lot of work into an art project, which I do, I wanted to do something more than just sit in a gallery with a very small audience of an art audience.

00:23:43.560 --> 00:23:52.070
 And it was sort of, you know, it was like, it was 2006, but there were, we were still feeling the wake of the invasion of Iraq.

00:23:52.070 --> 00:23:57.910
 And I was very involved in demonstrations during that time in San Francisco.

00:23:57.910 --> 00:24:02.370
 And that was one time where I was like, wow, this is, you know, there's still some politicized people here.

00:24:02.570 --> 00:24:07.620
 And that act galvanized a lot of people who hadn't really been together.

00:24:07.800 --> 00:24:11.130
 And one of those people I met in that time period was Matt Gonzalez.

00:24:12.020 --> 00:24:23.160
 And, um, his view on, on city politics was so inspiring that I was like, you know what, I actually want to do something for this city as an attempt to make it a livable city that I want to stay in.

00:24:23.160 --> 00:24:32.290
 Um, so it's kind of selfish, but, and so I had heard about victory gardens in a beautiful book called city bountiful by Laura Lawson.

00:24:32.290 --> 00:24:41.600
 And I think the coolest thing in her book was that she documented urban gardening projects and in a way where she wrote their successes and their failures.

00:24:41.600 --> 00:24:45.880
 And I thought it was so great to read failure so that you wouldn't duplicate them.

00:24:46.650 --> 00:24:57.050
 Um, but she outlined the victory garden program and it said in her book that San Francisco was the most, um, successful of all of the programs across the United States.

00:24:57.050 --> 00:25:03.200
 So I went to the history library in San Francisco and they had a folder called victory garden, very small one.

00:25:03.200 --> 00:25:13.230
 And in that folder was a picture by an anonymous, um, photographer of a farm in front of city hall, San Francisco, and a man standing in the foreground.

00:25:13.590 --> 00:25:18.840
 And all it said was that the man in the foreground was the director of park and recreation.

00:25:19.870 --> 00:25:24.800
 And, and I just was like, why don't we have a garden, a farm in front of city hall now?

00:25:24.920 --> 00:25:26.600
 Why do we have turf, you know?

00:25:27.520 --> 00:25:29.800
 So I had, I just kind of like a spark of idea.

00:25:29.900 --> 00:25:38.450
 I want to propose a, you know, contemporary version of victory gardens to the city of San Francisco and use my little stipend from the SF MoMA to give away some gardens.

00:25:38.450 --> 00:25:43.750
 And then also use some time to see if we can get the city to have a garden in front of city hall.

00:25:44.050 --> 00:25:52.130
 So I kind of knew Matt Gonzalez and I invited him to dinner and I told him my dream and I showed him the picture and he said, oh, come on, you have it.

00:25:52.770 --> 00:26:02.390
 Politicians, you know, if they can see what you're talking about and if it happened before you have historical precedent on your side, um, they can't imagine things that haven't happened yet.

00:26:02.390 --> 00:26:04.050
 So, you know, it'll totally work.

00:26:04.170 --> 00:26:09.070
 So he told me to go see Aaron Peskin, who was the president of board of supervisors.

00:26:10.320 --> 00:26:14.240
 And this is something I didn't know, but the supervisors have to give you time.

00:26:14.240 --> 00:26:17.720
 Like if you have an idea, they have to give you like seven minutes.

00:26:18.160 --> 00:26:22.470
 They can give you longer, but they can kick you out after seven minutes.

00:26:22.470 --> 00:26:29.150
 So I took this picture and I took a small proposal to Aaron Peskin and I asked, and Matt said I had to ask for three things.

00:26:29.150 --> 00:26:34.690
 One that was like a huge idea that they could never do so that they'd know I was like a dreamer.

00:26:34.910 --> 00:26:39.010
 One that was very concrete that they could do, but still challenging.

00:26:39.210 --> 00:26:41.350
 And then one that's like for sure they could do.

00:26:41.430 --> 00:26:45.030
 So I said, um, I would like to do a garden in front of city hall.

00:26:45.600 --> 00:26:46.900
 Um, he said, super fine.

00:26:46.940 --> 00:26:47.460
 Let's do it.

00:26:47.660 --> 00:26:50.670
 I want to have a permanent seed library in the library.

00:26:51.190 --> 00:26:57.840
 Um, and I want sustained funding, um, for urban farming over, you know, the millennia or whatever.

00:26:58.300 --> 00:26:59.340
 I said, okay, let's work on that.

00:26:59.340 --> 00:27:07.990
 Um, he said, but you know, let's bring a whole bunch of groups together and have a meeting next week because I'm sure lots of people will want to do this.

00:27:08.150 --> 00:27:10.890
 And so at that point I was like, whoa, this is happening.

00:27:11.170 --> 00:27:13.280
 I need a partner.

00:27:13.420 --> 00:27:13.980
 I'm an artist.

00:27:14.100 --> 00:27:15.960
 I'm not really a farmer, not really a politician.

00:27:15.960 --> 00:27:23.340
 So I went to the garden for the environment, which is my favorite urban garden and city funded garden that was started by slug.

00:27:23.520 --> 00:27:26.590
 Um, another great urban gardening project.

00:27:27.050 --> 00:27:30.960
 Um, and I asked Blair Randall, the director, if he'd want to partner on this.

00:27:31.020 --> 00:27:33.360
 And he immediately said, yes, like without a question.

00:27:33.360 --> 00:27:47.910
 So he waltzed into city hall a week later and there was 12 different representatives from like community land trust, uh, the department of, um, family and children, neighborhood alliance, park and rec.

00:27:48.070 --> 00:27:54.790
 You name it, Homeland security department was there and they all were so supportive.

00:27:54.790 --> 00:27:56.450
 Like they're like, you can have this land.

00:27:56.570 --> 00:27:57.450
 We'll give you money.

00:27:57.650 --> 00:28:05.550
 Homeland security said, we'll give you $200 and we can make bumper stickers that says be safe from the Taliban, have a victory garden.

00:28:07.270 --> 00:28:19.120
 Um, so Blair and I said, Hey, and everybody wanted to have a meeting like, like down the hall right away.

00:28:19.240 --> 00:28:23.380
 And we decided to take a week and think about what we wanted to do.

00:28:23.380 --> 00:28:41.540
 Um, and we proposed to meet with some of the people and do a garden in front of city hall and a program of backyard gardens for one year, um, to try to test out if people would really use city funding, um, to have backyard gardens, what were the challenges, et cetera.

00:28:41.960 --> 00:28:47.640
 Um, so we received $60,000 from the city to do a pilot program.

00:28:47.920 --> 00:28:55.430
 We partnered with slow food nation, um, to make a garden in front of city hall as part of their celebration.

00:28:55.930 --> 00:29:01.700
 Um, and then out of that spawned a lot of funding for workshops around the city.

00:29:01.880 --> 00:29:05.020
 Hayes Valley farm received a lot of support out of that.

00:29:05.020 --> 00:29:11.800
 And, um, I think the most exciting thing was really the, there was an urban food alliance group that formed out of that.

00:29:12.080 --> 00:29:15.880
 Um, which I think, you know, it still exists.

00:29:16.020 --> 00:29:17.140
 It still exists.

00:29:17.140 --> 00:29:26.300
 And I think it's put a lot of pressure and, um, just presence for the city to see that people are serious about farming in the city and around the Bay area.

00:29:26.400 --> 00:29:28.630
 And so, yeah, that was that project.

00:29:28.630 --> 00:29:34.080
 And it was so, I learned so much about so many things through that project.

00:29:34.360 --> 00:29:40.820
 Um, I'm like eternally grateful for the curator of the SF moment supporting that to be an artwork.

00:29:41.840 --> 00:29:56.420
 And well, something that stands out about that project to me is that being an artist seems like there was this possibility to catalyze all these different groups and aspects of society that maybe could have happened another way.

00:29:56.420 --> 00:30:06.300
 But I don't, I can't really imagine another way because it was like this experiment kind of, there was this utopic possibility there that people understand.

00:30:06.380 --> 00:30:08.740
 It was that people understood as an artwork, right?

00:30:09.090 --> 00:30:18.820
 But I don't know if they did, but yeah, I don't, I don't think still to this day, a lot of people understand it as an artwork.

00:30:19.020 --> 00:30:29.840
 It's, I'm often challenged, um, which I don't mind, but I think, you know, we were at the SF MoMA for a panel discussion when this happened.

00:30:29.840 --> 00:30:37.290
 And Matt was on a, Matt Gonzalez was one of the panel members with me and someone in the audience said, why is this an artwork?

00:30:37.950 --> 00:30:39.820
 And I wanted to defend it.

00:30:39.940 --> 00:30:44.550
 And Matt, I just, I saw Matt wanted to talk and he said, um, that's not an interesting question.

00:30:45.210 --> 00:30:46.350
 Let's ask the question.

00:30:46.450 --> 00:30:47.230
 Why is this relevant?

00:30:47.700 --> 00:30:49.500
 And I was like, ah, I love him.

00:30:49.500 --> 00:30:56.980
 You know, it was so good because, you know, when, if you ask that question, you have a way more interesting conversation than why is this art?

00:30:57.060 --> 00:31:05.750
 You just have the same recursive arts exchange that goes on in the art world, trying to figure out what art is over and over, which for me is not interesting at all.

00:31:06.550 --> 00:31:16.170
 Um, but if you're asking why it's relevant, you're, it's like to who, when, why, where, you know, it was the whole panel shifted and we talked about way more interesting things.

00:31:16.350 --> 00:31:17.680
 So, yeah.

00:31:18.040 --> 00:31:25.690
 Well, I, I love that when people are good at reframing things like that, actually make the conversation one that you want to be a part of.

00:31:26.230 --> 00:31:34.790
 Um, yeah, I was just talking to a photographer about how she, it doesn't tell anyone she's a photographer cause she doesn't want to tell them what kind of camera she uses.

00:31:35.550 --> 00:31:40.230
 Cause it's such a boring conversation for her, um, along the same lines, right.

00:31:40.290 --> 00:31:43.130
 Where it's like, who wants, who wants to have that conversation.

00:31:43.370 --> 00:31:51.440
 But then, so within that conversation, like within that reframing, like how do you talk about imagination or these places?

00:31:51.650 --> 00:31:57.700
 Like before you were describing this project, you were talking about how there's a lot of complexity in what you do.

00:31:57.920 --> 00:31:58.510
 Yeah.

00:31:58.510 --> 00:32:05.430
 And it, it doesn't always reach like a simple to describe a conclusive end.

00:32:05.640 --> 00:32:06.320
 No.

00:32:06.560 --> 00:32:06.780
 Right.

00:32:06.900 --> 00:32:09.500
 That's not what these, that's not what these projects do.

00:32:09.890 --> 00:32:10.310
 No.

00:32:10.510 --> 00:32:12.420
 So, so how do you talk about that?

00:32:12.480 --> 00:32:22.550
 Well, you have to have some, something concrete somewhere along the line, or you're just going to be floating around and not, not, you know, not taken seriously.

00:32:22.550 --> 00:32:29.430
 And so I think of what we're doing as a really big map and there's different points on it.

00:32:29.990 --> 00:32:40.760
 And those points need to be kind of, um, for us, their exhibitions, their publications, their moments where we distill what we're doing into something a little more graspable, graspable.

00:32:41.040 --> 00:33:00.380
 And as a, something to talk around, um, and I think, you know, when we make exhibitions, we often make sculptures that are somehow, it's almost a document of what we're doing, or like I said, a distillation or a tool that expresses what we're doing.

00:33:00.380 --> 00:33:09.150
 And the tool can be used or it could just be a static sculpture that provokes, that's an entry point into the work at large.

00:33:09.930 --> 00:33:16.730
 Um, and that's a real challenge, but it's also, it really helps us even to have a language around what we're doing.

00:33:16.730 --> 00:33:21.590
 And often our language is sculpture or, or publications.

00:33:21.730 --> 00:33:29.130
 Like we often use the form of an interview as a way to answer our own questions through other people.

00:33:29.130 --> 00:33:30.420
 You know what I mean?

00:33:31.710 --> 00:33:33.110
 I think that's what you do too.

00:33:33.470 --> 00:33:42.990
 Um, and, um, you know, we do a lot of public programs and they're sort of these moments where often, you know,

00:33:42.990 --> 00:33:48.540
 we're so fortunate to have the support from art, from cultural institutions to host these things.

00:33:48.540 --> 00:33:55.220
 Cause it's often at the perfect point where we're swimming around really complex questions and we need help to understand them.

00:33:55.220 --> 00:34:04.130
 So we're able to be able to bring, you know, specialists together, um, and, and, um, kind of frame what we're doing.

00:34:04.130 --> 00:34:06.860
 And that's, that's what I'm doing in London.

00:34:06.860 --> 00:34:14.650
 Like on Saturday, we're having a public program between a geneticist and a gorilla grain grower and a baker.

00:34:14.650 --> 00:34:20.670
 Um, and those three perspectives I think are really important because there's, you know,

00:34:20.860 --> 00:34:28.470
 the geneticist growing the pure race and the controlled lab, the gorilla farmer who I just helped harvest today in Ruskin park.

00:34:28.470 --> 00:34:32.810
 He, he grows ancient grain plots all over South London in public parks.

00:34:32.930 --> 00:34:33.710
 That's his farm.

00:34:33.710 --> 00:34:42.620
 Um, and he has a bakery, um, and he's often getting seeds from gene banks that haven't grown these seeds in, you know, 80 years.

00:34:42.620 --> 00:34:44.980
 And he'll, he'll be the first to grow them as a land race.

00:34:44.980 --> 00:34:48.600
 And then he'll send them back to the gene bank, you know, as contemporary seeds.

00:34:49.240 --> 00:34:56.490
 Um, and then a baker who's also growing grains, but who is mainly baking bread with these ancient grains.

00:34:56.490 --> 00:35:01.800
 So it's like, you know, academic, amateur and practitioner together.

00:35:02.060 --> 00:35:10.640
 And that, what I've brought them together to do is really kind of hash out what it means to grow these land races again.

00:35:11.130 --> 00:35:17.190
 How do we keep from selecting them into homogenous seeds as industrial seeds?

00:35:17.670 --> 00:35:21.170
 And, you know, like what's, what is a variety and who gets to decide on that?

00:35:21.170 --> 00:35:41.050
 Um, and I'm having them reflect on the seed journey as well as a kind of a point of thinking about how trade began and how the seed journey is a inflection of, of looking at the age of sale and trade and how that influenced so many things.

00:35:43.260 --> 00:35:53.350
 So why do you think it's important either for explaining your own work or, or in a bigger context to bring these different voices together to hash something out like that?

00:35:53.570 --> 00:36:01.860
 Um, maybe because we don't have a forum, like we're kind of these, like future farmers.

00:36:01.860 --> 00:36:08.560
 We, you know, we all teach or we work in other institutions as a way to survive.

00:36:09.670 --> 00:36:12.840
 Um, but I wouldn't say we have a really strong community.

00:36:13.120 --> 00:36:20.630
 Like if you, if you have a full-time job at any university, you kind of have access to that discourse or you can create it very easily.

00:36:21.730 --> 00:36:24.750
 Um, but we're floating around all the time.

00:36:25.310 --> 00:36:34.440
 So it's important for us to kind of create these, they're almost like temporary institutions or, um, temporary schools in a way.

00:36:34.440 --> 00:36:44.380
 Um, and I think for all of us in future farmers, we actually learn and teach much better in that environment where it's, it's a short term.

00:36:44.520 --> 00:36:50.610
 People are there because they want to be there curious and contributive and something comes out of it.

00:36:50.650 --> 00:36:55.570
 Um, each time that's for us really inspiring.

00:36:56.810 --> 00:37:03.080
 I really like when people, um, have to find some common language around something.

00:37:04.140 --> 00:37:06.000
 I've been thinking about that a lot.

00:37:06.000 --> 00:37:16.540
 This idea of sort of a choral performance rather than the, the idea that everybody should have, that commonality should be established as the first step.

00:37:16.980 --> 00:37:28.270
 Um, this idea of inviting people into the complexity of imagining these systems seems, seems like the possibility of, of a lot of what our world is right now.

00:37:28.270 --> 00:37:28.710
 Right.

00:37:29.100 --> 00:37:43.160
 Um, maybe, maybe one way, like, as you were talking, I was thinking part of me is like, no, we shouldn't all have a common language, but I just, I agree with you on, cause we need to be able to communicate.

00:37:43.160 --> 00:37:55.800
 But I think part of this project of looking at seeds and thinking about industrialization versus, you know, keeping diversity in our fruits stock is about variety.

00:37:56.490 --> 00:38:04.220
 And that's not about, you know, there's a commonality that a wheat is a wheat, but then there's all these varieties with different traits.

00:38:04.220 --> 00:38:06.160
 And I think that's what we want to hold on to.

00:38:06.600 --> 00:38:13.670
 And, um, I was just in Northern Norway and I took a yoiking workshop, which is a Sámi singing.

00:38:13.870 --> 00:38:15.550
 It's a Sámi song.

00:38:16.070 --> 00:38:21.850
 Like Sámi are the indigenous people of Northern Scandinavia and Russia, Siberia.

00:38:21.850 --> 00:38:30.030
 And basically everyone has a yoik in them and you, it's a way of storytelling, but it's this strange voice that you can kind of acquire.

00:38:30.370 --> 00:38:34.260
 And it was a two day workshop where the common thing was the song.

00:38:34.360 --> 00:38:39.850
 So we all sang the same song, but at the end of the workshop, we had to go around and sing it ourself.

00:38:39.850 --> 00:38:42.600
 And it was so nerve wracking, like to have to sing this.

00:38:42.740 --> 00:38:48.220
 And we all had such different ways of singing and compared to the first day to the second day,

00:38:48.220 --> 00:38:54.010
 how much our voice had evolved and how we became comfortable in our strangeness of this yoik.

00:38:55.870 --> 00:39:02.030
 But I was just, as everyone went and had, was nervous, awkward, sometimes beautiful.

00:39:02.350 --> 00:39:06.030
 I was just like, this is, this is what we want is this variety.

00:39:06.230 --> 00:39:09.870
 But there is this common framework of the song that keeps us together.

00:39:09.950 --> 00:39:15.470
 So I don't know if that's like resonating with you, but that's what it made me think of when you talked about this commonality.

00:39:16.570 --> 00:39:20.120
 Yeah, that's, that's exactly it.

00:39:20.220 --> 00:39:21.860
 Maybe I might have to use your story.

00:39:24.030 --> 00:39:25.630
 No, but the idea.

00:39:26.470 --> 00:39:42.400
 You helped me think of how I, because I also, I, we definitely need a common, a way to communicate, but I'm a little suspect of the word, you know, a common language because then it creates the sameness that we're trying to avoid.

00:39:42.560 --> 00:39:47.110
 I'm very, I'm very concerned with the idea of a common language as a goal.

00:39:47.660 --> 00:39:47.880
 Yeah.

00:39:48.180 --> 00:39:50.180
 I think we have a lot of common languages.

00:39:50.960 --> 00:39:51.160
 Yeah.

00:39:51.260 --> 00:39:58.550
 And I think we have too many common languages right now and not enough room for a variety, right?

00:39:58.550 --> 00:40:03.130
 I think the idea that like a wheat is a wheat is a wheat is, is not true.

00:40:03.760 --> 00:40:04.050
 Yeah.

00:40:04.050 --> 00:40:20.430
 And that there is actually something, there's something really visceral about the, about this kind of resuscitation of these ancient grains or ancient varieties that there are these like plural threads happening at the same time of survival and imagination.

00:40:20.430 --> 00:40:22.740
 And so many other things.

00:40:22.940 --> 00:40:23.180
 Yeah.

00:40:23.640 --> 00:40:31.050
 Anyways, I'm not really sure that where that was going, but it's, I think it was maybe helpful for you and I will see how it is for other listeners.

00:40:31.680 --> 00:40:32.860
 Yeah, yeah, exactly.

00:40:35.790 --> 00:40:40.660
 Um, well maybe, so you did victory gardens and then what happened after that?

00:40:40.700 --> 00:40:41.280
 Good question.

00:40:41.280 --> 00:40:50.240
 Um, I mean, it basically started framed me, especially as the urban gardening farm, farmer artist.

00:40:50.720 --> 00:41:06.590
 And we were in a lot of shows about farming and, but I think the most positive thing that came out of that was, um, the current project we've been working on for five years in Oslo, which is an urban farm with a centerpiece of an ancient grain field.

00:41:06.590 --> 00:41:16.640
 And a bakehouse, which is a permanent experimental architecture that's hosting three different types of bread ovens and a greenhouse and a seed library.

00:41:17.880 --> 00:41:21.520
 And it's in a common area in a new waterfront development.

00:41:21.990 --> 00:41:26.550
 And it was an area that was not quite figured out in the master plan.

00:41:26.770 --> 00:41:30.190
 In the master plan, it was supposed to be a sports field, but that was just like a placeholder.

00:41:31.260 --> 00:41:36.200
 Um, and when we were looking at their master plan, they were showing us all these different places we could do an artwork.

00:41:36.200 --> 00:41:37.460
 And we said, what about this place?

00:41:37.480 --> 00:41:41.240
 And they're like, oh, it's, you know, not desirable and it won't be developed for 20 years.

00:41:41.240 --> 00:41:43.790
 And we were like, why don't we go there and do something now?

00:41:44.800 --> 00:41:51.290
 Um, so we proposed that they should give away the land to citizens of Oslo to farm.

00:41:51.430 --> 00:41:54.770
 And they were like, no, no one's going to want to farm in the city.

00:41:55.010 --> 00:41:58.560
 And we said, put an ad out, put it in all these different languages.

00:41:59.120 --> 00:42:08.340
 And they agreed to give away a hundred boxes, the city, um, and 4,000 people signed up to get a box.

00:42:09.320 --> 00:42:11.180
 Um, and they were floored.

00:42:11.300 --> 00:42:12.460
 They were just like, no way.

00:42:13.030 --> 00:42:18.870
 And what that did is it, they opened up places and parks around Oslo to give away more boxes.

00:42:19.830 --> 00:42:22.830
 And Oslo was way behind San Francisco.

00:42:22.830 --> 00:42:29.870
 So it was sort of like being at the same time a victory garden where, you know, people were just starting to farm in the city.

00:42:30.430 --> 00:42:34.540
 And, um, I had a lot of tools under my belt from victory garden.

00:42:34.540 --> 00:42:35.980
 So I was like, let's go to the city.

00:42:36.100 --> 00:42:39.350
 Let's mobilize people who are farming.

00:42:40.250 --> 00:42:45.120
 Um, and we've had a farm now for four and a half years.

00:42:45.310 --> 00:42:51.570
 We partnered with the Norwegian farmers union and hired a farmer for three years, full-time farmer.

00:42:51.570 --> 00:42:59.520
 And we're hiring a baker with the bakers union to run the bakehouse and train people how to bake grain,

00:42:59.700 --> 00:43:03.000
 bake bread with these grains that are grown in the, on the farm.

00:43:03.900 --> 00:43:08.430
 Um, and so this has been, you know, a five year long project.

00:43:09.230 --> 00:43:12.550
 It's one more year and it's been like equally.

00:43:12.670 --> 00:43:20.160
 As mobilizing, you know, like to see how much the city embraced the project and how many people are involved.

00:43:20.600 --> 00:43:22.240
 Um, it's really central.

00:43:22.240 --> 00:43:26.700
 So people to stumble upon it, um, and more and more people are getting involved.

00:43:27.160 --> 00:43:30.850
 Um, so that's been like a main focus.

00:43:30.850 --> 00:43:34.510
 And then on the side, we've done a lot of exhibition, all kinds of exhibitions.

00:43:35.030 --> 00:43:43.280
 Um, and often I try to push out of people pigeonholing us as like the urban farm artists.

00:43:43.280 --> 00:43:45.880
 Cause I think we do a lot more than that.

00:43:46.140 --> 00:43:49.630
 What else, what else do you do?

00:43:50.290 --> 00:43:57.370
 Um, I think the, you know, there's a future farmers is a core group of five of us.

00:43:57.370 --> 00:44:01.310
 Myself, Michael Swain, who's a 22 year long collaborator.

00:44:01.920 --> 00:44:03.760
 Stain Skiffaliers is 10 years.

00:44:03.980 --> 00:44:04.820
 He's from Belgium.

00:44:05.180 --> 00:44:07.140
 Loda Branca is an architect from Belgium.

00:44:07.560 --> 00:44:11.580
 Marta van Dessel is a social artist from Antwerp.

00:44:12.450 --> 00:44:19.520
 Um, and myself and we form different constellations when we work.

00:44:19.520 --> 00:44:22.580
 And Michael Swain and I are the oldest collaborators.

00:44:22.580 --> 00:44:31.240
 And I think he and I, our question, our line of questioning is really, um, we're interested

00:44:31.240 --> 00:44:35.600
 in the tools that we make as humans and innovation and questioning innovation.

00:44:35.600 --> 00:44:39.040
 Cause I think he and I are both like makers, like let's make another thing.

00:44:39.040 --> 00:44:43.120
 You know, we're curious about design, redesigning things that don't function.

00:44:43.610 --> 00:44:52.090
 Um, we're excited about kind of reverse engineering things and finding new uses for, for tools that

00:44:52.090 --> 00:44:53.410
 are in our everyday life.

00:44:54.280 --> 00:45:01.180
 But we're also, you know, want to pause when we make things and say, does the world need

00:45:01.180 --> 00:45:02.670
 something more?

00:45:03.270 --> 00:45:06.090
 Do we need more things and why are we making?

00:45:06.270 --> 00:45:11.790
 And, you know, one project we did a couple of years ago was around the question of the

00:45:11.790 --> 00:45:17.510
 making of the atomic bomb and thinking about this as a, you know, a tool that changed our

00:45:17.510 --> 00:45:18.230
 lives forever.

00:45:18.860 --> 00:45:24.730
 And even biologically, you know, trees that grew after the testing of the atomic bomb, um,

00:45:25.070 --> 00:45:28.780
 botanists will say it's pre, a tree is pre-bomb or post-bomb.

00:45:29.280 --> 00:45:34.780
 So a tree holds and it's, it has an isotropic footprint of the atomic bomb that you can read.

00:45:35.820 --> 00:45:41.200
 Um, and to think about, you know, all the decisions that were made in that tool, that's, you know,

00:45:41.200 --> 00:45:42.780
 it's quite a big tool to question.

00:45:43.690 --> 00:45:50.910
 Um, but we ended up for that project, we made a series of nails for Robert Oppenheimer because

00:45:50.910 --> 00:45:57.520
 we found some notes from the unclassified, uh, letters in his office where he,

00:45:57.660 --> 00:46:01.160
 when he was setting up his office in Los Alamos, he sent a letter out saying,

00:46:02.130 --> 00:46:05.620
 I need a, you know, light bulb and I need a nail for my hat.

00:46:05.740 --> 00:46:11.100
 And then in the same day, he sent out another letter and said, thanks for the light bulb.

00:46:11.240 --> 00:46:13.840
 Thanks for the coat rack, but I still need a nail for my hat.

00:46:13.840 --> 00:46:18.950
 And we were like, okay, he's in the middle of setting up the most huge project.

00:46:18.950 --> 00:46:20.010
 He's recruiting physicists.

00:46:20.010 --> 00:46:22.730
 He's, you know, building housing for laborers.

00:46:22.730 --> 00:46:25.800
 And he's, he's so fixed on this nail for his hat.

00:46:25.900 --> 00:46:28.380
 We were like, let's make a nail for Mr. Oppenheimer.

00:46:29.580 --> 00:46:36.700
 Um, and we tried to make a nail with just what we knew and our network of people we knew.

00:46:37.290 --> 00:46:41.090
 Um, and that took us on a whole journey where we ended up making three nails.

00:46:41.250 --> 00:46:42.870
 And I don't think I'll go into all the details.

00:46:42.870 --> 00:46:44.260
 You can look it up online.

00:46:44.360 --> 00:46:46.560
 The project was called For Want of a Nail.

00:46:47.840 --> 00:46:56.620
 Um, but it led us through material questioning, uh, history of toolmaking, history of materials

00:46:56.620 --> 00:46:59.390
 that we've used in making tools.

00:46:59.970 --> 00:47:05.210
 And yeah, a lot of questions about innovation.

00:47:09.260 --> 00:47:12.040
 So this is where these questions take you.

00:47:12.120 --> 00:47:13.880
 Sometimes you end up making three nails.

00:47:14.100 --> 00:47:14.340
 Yeah.

00:47:14.760 --> 00:47:16.900
 That sounds pretty good.

00:47:17.120 --> 00:47:19.520
 So you're not, you're not just an urban farmer.

00:47:19.700 --> 00:47:22.250
 No, I think we're tool makers.

00:47:23.290 --> 00:47:24.970
 That's, isn't that what we are as humans?

00:47:26.540 --> 00:47:27.980
 Yeah, I think so.

00:47:32.410 --> 00:47:33.530
 Tools for fools.

00:47:33.830 --> 00:47:34.370
 I don't know.

00:47:35.260 --> 00:47:36.400
 Tools for fools.

00:47:37.540 --> 00:47:44.150
 Well, why don't, why don't we, um, wrap it up and you tell me how we can follow along

00:47:44.150 --> 00:47:50.260
 with Seed Journey things and, um, anything else we should know about what's coming up next.

00:47:50.420 --> 00:47:50.600
 Okay.

00:47:51.360 --> 00:47:56.000
 So, um, Seed Journey is leaving from Oslo on September 17th.

00:47:56.000 --> 00:48:01.220
 You can follow on futurefarmers.com forward slash Seed Journey.

00:48:01.800 --> 00:48:06.440
 We are sailing on an 1895 wooden sailboat.

00:48:06.760 --> 00:48:12.680
 It's a Norwegian rescue boat that was designed by Colin Archer, who was a famous shipwright,

00:48:12.830 --> 00:48:19.200
 who also designed Fridjof Nansen's Fromm, who was the first boat to make it to the North Pole.

00:48:20.310 --> 00:48:22.090
 Um, it's a hardy boat.

00:48:22.480 --> 00:48:30.690
 We have a crew of 16 different artists and anthropologists that will be rotating on and off the boat over the course of the year.

00:48:31.210 --> 00:48:39.720
 And when we end in Istanbul, we're hosted by Salt Galata, which is an amazing, um, cultural institution in Istanbul.

00:48:39.960 --> 00:48:46.040
 And there we will have a gathering of all the crew members and a small exhibition and public program.

00:48:48.220 --> 00:48:53.060
 And if you'd like to support the project in any way, our website has a donation page.

00:48:53.280 --> 00:48:59.480
 And we're still seeking funding to make this all happen, but we're going nonetheless because the wind is blowing.

00:49:01.680 --> 00:49:02.040
 Perfect.

00:49:02.700 --> 00:49:05.480
 And you can listen to the radio station, right?

00:49:05.580 --> 00:49:05.760
 Yeah.

00:49:07.040 --> 00:49:07.840
 Radio Ramona.

00:49:08.060 --> 00:49:08.540
 Radio Ramona.

00:49:09.520 --> 00:49:11.280
 I just listened to it before we talked.

00:49:12.490 --> 00:49:12.810
 Yeah.

00:49:14.090 --> 00:49:14.570
 Yeah.

00:49:14.810 --> 00:49:15.390
 Well, thanks.

00:49:15.590 --> 00:49:17.910
 Thanks so much, Amy, for making the time today.

00:49:17.910 --> 00:49:18.690
 Thank you for me.

00:49:18.910 --> 00:49:20.830
 Nice to talk to you and making these tools.

